The board contains exactly three open 4-in-a-row lines for \(x\) and only one open 4-in-a-row line for \(o\):

- \(x\) can still finish the horizontal on row 1, columns 3-6 (currently two \(x\) and two empty cells at \((1,5)\) and \((1,6)\));
- \(x\) can still finish the horizontal on row 0, columns 0-3;
- \(x\) can still finish the vertical in column 0, rows 0-3;
- \(o\) can still finish the diagonal \((0,6)\)-\((1,5)\)-\((2,4)\)-\((3,3)\).

No other 4-cell window is free of the opposing colour, so these four lines are the only ones that can ever produce a win.

The unique empty cells that belong to \(o\)'s line are precisely \((1,5)\) and \((0,6)\). Consequently any move that occupies one of those two cells permanently kills \(o\)'s only winning chance. The cell \((1,5)\) is currently the lowest empty cell of its column, so it is immediately playable.

Playing column 5 therefore does two things at once:

- it occupies \((1,5)\), eliminating \(o\)'s diagonal forever;
- it converts \(x\)'s own row-1 line into an immediate threat (three \(x\) and an open cell at the playable square \((1,6)\)).

Black is forced to answer in column 6; after that forced exchange both of the right-hand lines are dead and it is again \(x\)'s turn. The remaining empty cells consist of the three left-hand columns that contain \(x\)'s two surviving lines plus two harmless “null” cells at the tops of columns 5 and 6. With the first-move advantage on that residual board \(x\) can always claim at least one of the two left-hand lines or, against perfect defence, force a complete filling of the board with neither player obtaining four. In other words the position is a draw with optimal play.

Every other legal opening lets \(o\) occupy \((1,5)\) on the next move. After that occupation the two cells of column 6 become a forced win for \(o\) by a simple parity argument on the remaining safe cells; \(x\) cannot prevent the loss. Consequently columns 0, 1, 2 and 6 are all losing moves, while column 5 is the unique move that secures the draw.

Final Answer: 5

